# Sequence Diagram — Encode-and-Close, and Ongoing → Closed
# Paste into https://play.d2lang.com/
#
# REV 4: replaces 08-sequence-assign-resolve.d2, which modelled an
#        assign → resolve → close workflow the department does not run.

shape: sequence_diagram

staff: IT Staff
ui: Web App
api: Application API
guard: Auth / RBAC Guard
svc: Ticket Service
gen: Ticket Number Generator
audit: Audit Logger
ob: Sync Outbox
db: PostgreSQL
q: Redis Queue

fix: Work happens before the ticket exists {
  staff -> staff: "receives concern, fixes it\n(or cannot finish it now)"
}

encode: Encode the outcome {
  staff -> ui: "open encoding form"
  ui -> staff: "status defaults to Closed\n(the common case)"
  staff -> ui: "employee, department, main issue,\nconcern, remarks, status"
  ui -> api: "POST /tickets"
  api -> guard: "check role"
  guard -> api: "permitted"
  api -> svc: "encode(cmd)"

  svc -> db: "BEGIN"
  svc -> gen: "nextNumber(date)"
  gen -> db: "lock + increment scope_key"
  db -> gen: "last_sequence"
  gen -> svc: "ticket_no"

  alt: {
    solved_on_the_spot: {
      svc.a: "status = Closed\nclosed_at = now()\nongoing_at stays NULL"
      svc -> db: "INSERT ticket (status='Closed',\nclosed_at = now())"
      svc.b: "ongoing_at IS NULL on a Closed\nticket = first-time fix"
    }
    could_not_finish: {
      svc.c: "status = Ongoing\nongoing_at = now()"
      svc -> db: "INSERT ticket (status='Ongoing',\nongoing_at = now())"
    }
    not_yet_attempted: {
      svc.d: "status = Open\nno timestamps set"
      svc -> db: "INSERT ticket (status='Open')"
    }
  }

  svc -> audit: "log(CREATE)"
  audit -> db: "INSERT audit_log"
  svc -> ob: "enqueue UPSERT"
  ob -> db: "INSERT sync_outbox"
  svc -> db: "COMMIT"
  svc -> q: "dispatch SyncTicketJob"
  api -> ui: "201 Created"
  ui -> staff: "ticket_no"
}

later: Close an Ongoing ticket, later {
  staff -> ui: "open ticket, add remarks, close"
  ui -> api: "PATCH /tickets/{id}/close"
  api -> guard: "check role"
  api -> svc: "close(ticket_id, remarks)"
  svc -> db: "BEGIN"
  svc -> db: "SELECT ... FOR UPDATE"
  db -> svc: "current state = Ongoing"
  svc.check: "legal? Open→Closed and\nOngoing→Closed are the only\npaths into Closed (see 07)"
  svc -> db: "UPDATE status='Closed',\nremarks = :remarks, closed_at = now()"
  svc -> audit: "log(STATUS_CHANGE,\n'Ongoing' → 'Closed')"
  svc -> audit: "log(UPDATE, remarks)"
  audit -> db: "INSERT audit_log x2\n(one row per changed field)"
  svc -> ob: "enqueue UPSERT"
  svc -> db: "COMMIT"
  svc -> q: "dispatch SyncTicketJob"
  api -> ui: "200 OK"
}

reject: Closed is terminal {
  staff -> ui: "problem came back"
  ui -> staff: "Closed tickets do not reopen.\nEncode a new ticket."
  staff -> ui: "encode new ticket → back to top"
}

note: |md
  **Why the first block has no system in it**

  The department fixes the problem, then writes it down. The ticket is a record, not
  a work order. This is why the form defaults to Closed and why creation can enter
  any of the three states directly — forcing every ticket through Open would add
  clicks to record something already finished.

  **The pattern every mutation follows**

  Row lock → legality check → update → audit → outbox → commit → dispatch.

  1. **`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` before the transition check.** Without the lock, two
     users acting on one ticket can both read `Ongoing`, both judge their transition
     legal, and both write — last writer wins, and the audit log shows a transition
     that never happened.
  2. **The state machine in `07-ticket-state-machine.d2` is enforced in code.** The
     three statuses are not a free-text field the UI happens to constrain.
  3. **Audit writes one row per changed field**, not one per request. Closing an
     Ongoing ticket with remarks changes two fields, so it emits two rows.
  4. **The outbox row commits with the change** — never after, never separately.
  5. **The queue job dispatches after commit.** Dispatching inside the transaction
     races the worker: it can pick up the job and query a row that hasn't landed yet.

  **Assignment is not in this diagram.** The technician list records who handled the
  concern (ADR-0017); it is data on the ticket, not a lifecycle stage. Nothing branches
  on it, and Closed tickets remain re-assignable — correcting the record is an edit
  (FR-9), not a reopen.
| {near: bottom-center; shape: text}
